    {
      "parent": 70,
      "role": "AXTextField",
      "description": "Flow source",
      "value": "// The compensation showcase for REQ-AUTO-4.\n//\n// This flow is not a migration of anything: it is the fixture that exercises the\n// abort policy `onFailure=compensate:<story>`, which no legacy flow could express.\n// It reuses execution.flow's scenario names and steps so the two read together,\n// and adds the one scenario the original never had — \"cancel booking\" — as the\n// compensating story.\n//\n// The first migration of execution.flow folded this into that file, which cost it\n// three of its original scenario names and added a scenario the original did not\n// contain. Keeping the showcase here leaves execution.flow a faithful migration\n// (P0-F2).\n\nscenario (onFailure=compensate:cancel booking): book a slot\n  Click the Book a slot link\n  Click the location field\n  Type \"indra\" into the location field\n  Click the Indiranagar suggestion\n  Click the next button\n  Click the Book now button\n\nscenario (onFailure=compensate:cancel booking): pay for the slot\n  Click the booking result\n  Click the Checkout link\n  Type {data.card.number} into the card number field\n  Select \"08\" in the expiry month select\n  Select \"2027\" in the expiry year select\n  Type {data.card.cvv} into the CVV field\n  Click the pay button\n\n// The compensating story. It runs with the failing flow's scope and then the\n// flow stops (LLD §8.3), so a booking created before the failure is not left\n// behind.\nscenario (idempotent): cancel booking\n  Click the Bookings link\n  Click the cancel booking button\n\ncompose: book and pay\n  book a slot\n  pay for the slot\n\ntest: book and pay\n",
      "enabled": true,
      "actions": [
        "AXConfirm",
        "AXSetValue"
      ],
      "box": [
        16,
        157,
        616,
        376
      ],
      "domIdentifier": "flow-text"
